Lab Tasks
Task 1: Define REST Endpoints Using REST DSL
Subtask 1.1: Configure REST Configuration
Create a new Java class RestServiceRouteBuilder.java
Add basic REST configuration:
import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;

public class RestServiceRouteBuilder extends RouteBuilder {
    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {
        // REST configuration
        restConfiguration()
            .component("jetty")
            .host("localhost")
            .port(8080)
            .bindingMode(RestBindingMode.json);
    }
}
Expected Outcome: Basic REST service setup with Jetty server on port 8080 configured for JSON binding.

Subtask 1.2: Define REST Endpoint
Extend the previous configuration with a sample endpoint:
rest("/api")
    .description("Sample REST service")
    .consumes("application/json")
    .produces("application/json")
    
    .get("/hello")
        .description("Simple hello endpoint")
        .to("direct:hello");
Add a route to handle the request:
from("direct:hello")
    .transform().constant("Hello from Camel REST service");
Expected Outcome: A GET endpoint at http://localhost:8080/api/hello that returns a simple message.

Task 2: Implement HTTP Methods
Subtask 2.1: Create GET Method
Add a GET endpoint to retrieve data:
rest("/api")
    .get("/products/{id}")
        .description("Get product by ID")
        .outType(Product.class)
        .to("direct:getProduct");

from("direct:getProduct")
    .process(exchange -> {
        String id = exchange.getIn().getHeader("id", String.class);
        // In real app, fetch from database
        Product product = new Product(id, "Sample Product", 99.99);
        exchange.getMessage().setBody(product);
    });
Note: Create a Product.java class with id, name, and price fields.

Subtask 2.2: Create POST Method
Add a POST endpoint:
rest("/api")
    .post("/products")
        .description("Create new product")
        .type(Product.class)
        .outType(Product.class)
        .to("direct:createProduct");

from("direct:createProduct")
    .process(exchange -> {
        Product product = exchange.getIn().getBody(Product.class);
        // In real app, save to database
        product.setId("generated-" + System.currentTimeMillis());
        exchange.getMessage().setBody(product);
    });
Subtask 2.3: Create PUT and DELETE Methods
Add PUT and DELETE endpoints:
rest("/api")
    .put("/products/{id}")
        .description("Update product")
        .type(Product.class)
        .to("direct:updateProduct")
        
    .delete("/products/{id}")
        .description("Delete product")
        .to("direct:deleteProduct");

from("direct:updateProduct")
    .process(exchange -> {
        String id = exchange.getIn().getHeader("id", String.class);
        Product updatedProduct = exchange.getIn().getBody(Product.class);
        // Update logic here
        exchange.getMessage().setBody("Product " + id + " updated");
    });

from("direct:deleteProduct")
    .process(exchange -> {
        String id = exchange.getIn().getHeader("id", String.class);
        // Delete logic here
        exchange.getMessage().setBody("Product " + id + " deleted");
    });
Expected Outcome: Complete CRUD operations available at /api/products endpoints.

Task 3: Implement Data Formats
Subtask 3.1: JSON Support
The previous examples already use JSON binding. To make it explicit:
restConfiguration()
    .component("jetty")
    .host("localhost")
    .port(8080)
    .bindingMode(RestBindingMode.json)
    .dataFormatProperty("prettyPrint", "true");
Subtask 3.2: XML Support
Add XML binding support:
restConfiguration()
    // ... existing config
    .bindingMode(RestBindingMode.auto);

rest("/api")
    .get("/products/xml/{id}")
        .produces("application/xml")
        .outType(Product.class)
        .to("direct:getProductXml");

// Add JAXB annotation to Product class
@XmlRootElement
public class Product {
    // ... fields and methods
}
Troubleshooting Tip: If XML doesn't work, ensure camel-jaxb dependency is included and classes are properly annotated.

Testing the Services
Start the Camel context:
public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    CamelContext context = new DefaultCamelContext();
    context.addRoutes(new RestServiceRouteBuilder());
    context.start();
    Thread.sleep(5 * 60 * 1000);
    context.stop();
}
Test endpoints using curl:
# GET
curl http://localhost:8080/api/hello

# POST
curl -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"name":"New Product","price":29.99}' http://localhost:8080/api/products

# XML
curl -H "Accept: application/xml" http://localhost:8080/api/products/xml/123
Conclusion
In this lab, you have:

Configured a Camel REST service using the REST DSL
Implemented all major HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)
Set up automatic JSON and XML data binding
Created a complete CRUD REST API with Camel
Key Takeaways:

Camel's REST DSL provides a fluent API for defining REST endpoints
Binding modes handle automatic conversion between Java objects and data formats
REST endpoints can be easily connected to existing Camel routes
Multiple data formats can be supported simultaneously
